00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.120
We took all these various
best practices associated

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.790
with how we do things and
applied it to today's world,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.890
an open sources project
called Kubernetes,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.060
that's run today by the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.610
It's 100% open source, written
in Go, and it'll run anywhere.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.586
So these are things to
remember about Kubernetes.

00:03:14.586 --> 00:03:16.460
For those of you that
are moving to the cloud

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.015
and going down
that journey, this

00:03:18.015 --> 00:03:21.680
is an opportunity to
really have a central way

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.270
of doing things and deploying
these hermetically type

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:27.980
environments that
are very predictable

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.820
across different environments.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.210
So as you all know,
containers are great.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.840
They add a lot of value, but
typically after the first five

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.730
hours, you encounter challenges
around scale, health checks.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.880
How do you get containers
networked together,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.650
and how do you
operate an environment

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:51.200
and ensure you're able to push
things into production quickly,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.840
and do rolling updates, and
all these various things.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.680
How do you take
containers that typically

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.240
don't have any notion of data,
and attach volumes to them,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.580
and manage the entire life
cycle of these things?

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.430
This is where
Kubernetes comes in,

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.980
and it provides this
environment, this platform

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.380
for you, in terms of running and
managing containers at scale.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:19.700
So this was my 101 two-minute
on Kubernetes, assuming

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.210
most of you already understand
bits and pieces of it,

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.560
and you're interested
mostly in around,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.470
how do you manage
different clusters

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.250
across different teams?

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.450
So the typical Kubernetes
journey usually starts with,

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.820
hey, we're getting
into containers,

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.200
we need an orchestrator.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.230
Let's find an
application that we

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.600
think makes a good
fit for containers,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.960
and let's go ahead and
do proof of concept.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.060
You identify a
group of developers.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.360
They take their
application, they

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.050
containerize, they
run it on Kubernetes.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.360
A month later you show it
to the rest of the team

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.950
and everybody's like, this
is cool, this is awesome.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.060
How do we get some of this?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:03.780
Another team comes
along and says, hey,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.320
we want access to this cluster.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.110
So in some cases you'd let
them spin up their own cluster,

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:13.760
in other cases you basically
give them access to the sandbox

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.130
cluster that they
go to town with

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.481
and try and run their
applications and so on.

00:05:19.481 --> 00:05:21.230
Then you get to the
point where, OK, we're

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:22.370
ready to go to production.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:23.200
Now what?

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:23.780
Right?

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.570
There's some choices that you
have to make at this point,

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:30.680
and some of you I've talked
to are facing these things.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.240
One choice is-- do I
create a project per team?

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.460
Do I create a cluster per team?

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.370
Do I run one shared
cluster and have

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:43.460
teams run their own namespace?

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:44.330
What do I do?

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.720
I have requirements that
have different regulatory

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:48.459
requirements.

00:05:48.459 --> 00:05:50.000
Do I put them in a
different cluster,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.850
in a different project?

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.050
How should I look at
these requirements,

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:57.200
and what's the best practice
associated with that?

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.350
There's really no
single perfect answer.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.660
It all comes down to how your
organization's structured,

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.010
how your teams are structured,
and really, the requirements

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:10.980
of the application.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.735
So what I want to
do is just walk you

00:06:12.735 --> 00:06:15.760
through some examples of
things that we see out there

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.020
and then provide you our opinion
around some best practices.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.960
So one common example
we see out there

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.540
is you have one
project and one cluster

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.440
and then different name
spaces, and these name spaces

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:32.720
map to different
applications, different teams.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.990
So we see quite a bit of that.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:37.285
The pros is it's
very easy to manage.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:44.130
It's a single cluster context
that you're dealing with,

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.780
and you're basically running
all your applications

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:49.570
using that single context.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.400
There's cost savings
associated with it.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.580
You're taking advantage of
the namespace for isolation.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:58.210
So you're able to pack more
resources into that one

00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.720
cluster.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.680
So you're getting some
potential cost savings.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.380
Your resource
isolation is really

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:09.460
based on the namespace
quota that you're defining,

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.780
and we'll talk a bit
about that later on.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.260
And then you can take advantage
of some of the features

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:18.820
that we'll talk about later that
we introduced in Kubernetes 1.6

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:23.730
that applies role based access
control to the namespace

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.150
itself.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:28.690
Some of the cons associated
with that is you're

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.840
limited in terms of
what you can isolate

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.480
from a namespace perspective--

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.080
compute and memory.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.300
So if you want to isolate
with more resources,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.330
these things are
not available yet.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.510
And really, you're basically
leveraging that quota

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:51.490
on that namespace as
the only parameter

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:53.080
for doing the isolation.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:55.040
So that's one example.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.140
Another example is you have
one project but then different

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:00.140
clusters.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:03.700
And this one we
actually see the most.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:08.270
So in this particular case,
you have a staging cluster,

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.990
or potentially like a
Def test staging cluster,

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.330
and then you have
a prod cluster.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:15.880
And within each one
of these clusters

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.380
you have different
applications that

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:19.410
run in their own namespace.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.600
Some pros with this is
you now have the ability

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.260
to isolate some of
the cluster resources.

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:30.700
Like your master
is isolated from

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:36.530
the masters for
staging and test.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:41.140
You're not exposing your master
to potential DDoS attack that

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.929
could happen all of a sudden.

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:47.766
You have the ability now to
run two different networks.

00:08:47.766 --> 00:08:49.390
So you're not sharing
that same network

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.830
across these two different
operating environments.

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:58.960
You can basically have dedicated
virtual machines and DNS

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.500
service for each one
of these environments.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.780
So this is something that
we see quite a bit of

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:09.880
and typically it meets,
say, 80% of the requirements

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.880
for companies that
we're talking to.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.630
And then the other important
one from advantages,

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:19.780
a cluster can be
deployed in a single zone

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.740
or it can be deployed
across multiple zones.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:25.210
So in prod you might want to
deploy across multiple zones.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:28.520
In dev tests you probably want
to deploy in a single zone.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.440
So you have the ability to
assign different availability

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.740
characteristics across
these cluster environments.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:40.016
Disadvantage,
again, you lose some

00:09:40.016 --> 00:09:41.140
of the management benefits.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:42.940
So you're managing
two different clusters

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:48.190
and you have to manage across
different contexts and so on.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.310
The third case is really
different project,

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:56.500
different cluster, and
different namespace.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.520
This is something that we
see with some of the bigger

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.150
organizations that want to put
their production environment

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.160
in a separate project.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.910
And the advantage for that
is you have your own quotas,

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.740
and these quotas really are
your compute engine quotas

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.990
that you can
allocate specifically

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.420
for that particular project.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.830
Somebody running
dev test staging

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.080
can't come along and
consume your quota.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.660
The next thing you know your
application has a spike.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.750
And you're calling your cloud
provider, and you're like, hey,

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.520
I need for you to
increase my limits.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.590
And you're like, these
things take time.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.661
Another advantage is if
you're doing chargeback.

00:10:36.661 --> 00:10:38.160
Today, unfortunately,
there's really

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.890
no way within a cluster
to do chargeback

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:47.380
based on applications that are
running within that cluster.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:51.060
So you'd have to run
it in its own project

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:53.910
and then do chargeback
based on the billing

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.830
that you get for that
particular project.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:57.496
In the future this
is an area that we're

00:10:57.496 --> 00:11:00.230
very interested in
addressing, but for today,

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.240
if you have requirement
around chargeback,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:05.170
run it in its own project.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:09.390
And then greater control around
identity and access management,

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.350
definitely.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.904
And again, like
the previous slide,

00:11:13.904 --> 00:11:15.570
you lose some of the
management benefits

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.300
because now you're dealing
with multiple projects,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.720
multiple clusters.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.720
But you can take advantage
of some of the things

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.020
that we've introduced
around cross project

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.820
networking and all
these various things

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.740
that we're doing in
Google Cloud Platform that

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.430
are very beneficial.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:33.690
So suggested best practice
is really run your production

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:37.950
environment separate project,
put your dev test staging

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.830
in their own project,
split them up

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.200
based on clusters
depending on your needs,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.560
and then use namespaces to
break out your applications

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:47.490
within that cluster.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.664
And here's just a list
of the recommendations

00:11:52.664 --> 00:11:53.580
that I just mentioned.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.400
So most customers create
cluster per environment,

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.790
and then this is just a
repeat of what I just said.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:04.610
Complete isolation
across namespace

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:07.580
is something that's
still a work in progress.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:11.090
We'd love to hear your feedback
on some of your requirements

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:11.770
in this space.

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:13.520
So after the talk
please tell us.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:20.280
So let's talk about resource
control and resource

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.240
management.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:24.150
So most of you probably
understand what a pod is,

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:28.560
but for those of you
that don't, any time

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.970
you have containers
that potentially need

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:39.090
to be tightly coupled, or share
a network, or share volumes,

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.340
you put those
containers in a pod.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.130
So pod is this
architectural pattern

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:46.080
that was introduced
in Kubernetes

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.710
that really is about how you
run and deploy containers.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.720
It is a unit of scale.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.770
Everything that you run in
Kubernetes runs inside a pod.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.630
And when your
application scales,

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.730
it is these pods
that are actually

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:03.490
scaling across the nodes
within your cluster.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:08.570
So everything within a pod
lives and dies together,

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:13.390
and basically it's how you
deploy your application

00:13:13.390 --> 00:13:14.700
in Kubernetes.

00:13:17.889 --> 00:13:19.680
What we've done with
Kubernetes is give you

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.250
the ability to isolate
some of these resources.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.980
And today we have
various compute resources

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.980
that we expose to enable
you to do some isolation

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.400
terms of the containers that
you're deploying in Kubernetes.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.790
So some examples
are CPU and memory.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:39.900
So based on your application's
CPU and memory requirements,

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.170
you can specify these when
you create your containers,

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.290
and then the scheduler
will automatically

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.230
apply the right quality of
service for your application,

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:52.500
depending on how much CPU
and memory that you require.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:55.220
In the future we're looking
at also extending this

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.690
to local storage.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:01.620
So why would you need
strong isolation for pods?

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.860
Well, suppose you have
certain applications

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.690
that are running in
the same cluster,

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.790
but you want to
ensure that they don't

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.670
interfere with each other.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.650
You would isolate your
pods, and this goes back

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.830
to the whole notion of cgroups.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.650
Those of you that
use Google Cloud

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:24.210
and sometimes understand the
fact that our virtual machine

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.510
spin up very quickly, it's
because our virtual machines

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:29.730
actually run inside containers.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:35.520
And the fact that you don't
see a lot of noisy neighbor

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.790
issues with Google Cloud
Platform, and a lot of this

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.290
has some of the
benefits associated

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:42.990
with using cgroups and so on.

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.610
Better predictability.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:47.490
By isolating your
pods, you're actually

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:49.260
able to-- for
certain applications

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.650
that you want guaranteed, be
able to specify that and ensure

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.900
that they're always
going to be able to run.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.510
A web serving application
and a monitoring application

00:14:57.510 --> 00:14:59.470
are critical for your business.

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:05.670
So you can't handle a situation
where you can't schedule a web

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.020
service application.

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:09.960
Some of the cons
associated with it

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:13.020
would be oftentimes
you don't know

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.170
what your application needs.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:18.670
Perhaps you don't have
the historical data,

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:22.290
and it's not trivial to actually
figure these things out.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:26.220
We've tried to make it easier,
but oftentimes you just

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:31.680
have to start with, perhaps,
no isolation and then learn

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:33.250
as your application's running.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:36.000
Look at cases where you
have evictions, and based

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.580
on these evictions, figure
out, and tune, and tweak,

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:41.310
and figure out what the
right limits for setting

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:44.430
for your pods and containers.

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:48.810
And then utilization--
when you isolate,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:52.740
you're actually perhaps
reducing your efficiency.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.140
Because you're asking
for guarantees,

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:59.130
and your application
may not take advantage

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:00.150
of all these guarantees.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:04.290
So there might be some
unused resources that end up

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:06.510
lowering your efficiency.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.910
So we have this
concept of what's

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.170
called a request and limit.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.360
So when you create a
container you basically

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:17.670
specify a request, and a request
is how much resource, actually,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.980
does my application need.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:26.150
And you specify
this in CPU and RAM.

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:27.720
That's pretty much it.

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:31.050
So you can actually
specify these things

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.360
at container creation time.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:38.940
Now, suppose that you want to
oversubscribe or overcommit.

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:41.100
We have this other
parameter, actually,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.660
that's called a limit.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:47.190
And if you set your limit
greater than your request,

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.370
then you're actually
overpromising.

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:54.480
Similar to an airline
that overbooks and assumes

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.737
not everybody is going
to show up to the gate,

00:16:56.737 --> 00:16:58.320
and if everybody
shows up to the gate,

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:01.740
someone is going to get
pushed to the next plane

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.560
or given a free ticket.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.200
So these two parameters,
request and limit,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.720
are things that you can
configure at the container

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:10.470
level.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:15.930
Now, a pod actually inherits
all these requests and limits

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.740
that are defined at
the container level.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:23.430
So a pod request becomes the sum
of all the container requests,

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:28.270
and a pod limit becomes the sum
of all the container limits.

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:28.890
OK.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.740
So based on your
request and limits,

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:35.265
the scheduler actually figures
out what the quality of service

00:17:35.265 --> 00:17:37.200
is for your pod.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:42.030
So if you set your
request greater than zero,

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:44.850
but you set your request
equal to your limit--

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.190
so you don't want
overcommit, you

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:48.780
don't want oversubscription.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.570
You set those two
values as equal.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:55.760
Your pod is actually going
to be guaranteed to run.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:01.280
Now, if you set your request
to be a certain value

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:05.210
but then set your limit
greater than your request,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:07.250
then your pod is going
to get what's called

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:09.500
a burstable quality of service.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.910
Meaning that if there are
no available resources,

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.675
it will do best effort.

00:18:15.675 --> 00:18:20.270
It will try to deploy
this pod on these nodes,

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:22.220
but there's no real guarantee.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:25.220
Now, by default, if you
don't specify anything

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.650
and you just keep
your requests at zero

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:31.160
or don't provide any
value, then your pods

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.124
are scheduled what's
called best effort.

00:18:33.124 --> 00:18:34.790
And they're the ones
that will typically

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:40.740
get evicted first when you
don't have enough resources.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:45.500
So in the order of eviction,
it's best effort, burstable,

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:46.910
and then guaranteed.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:54.720
Now, your namespaces also
have requests and limits

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:56.230
that you can specify.

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.260
So you provide these
requests and limits

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.850
on the namespace level.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:04.920
And what actually happens
is now all of the requests

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.440
and limits associated with
all pods that are running

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:11.010
within that namespace, you're
guaranteeing that they will not

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.320
exceed that quota limit
that you're specifying

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:14.610
at the namespace level.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:18.930
So a good use case is if you
have different teams that

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:22.530
are sharing a cluster, they
each have their own namespace.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:24.870
One team you know
traditionally has

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:28.380
gone off and done some
things that literally

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:32.280
are unpredictable, and gone
off and did some resource

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:33.030
utilization.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:40.800
They haven't shared much
insight with you in the past.

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:44.310
Well, you can set a quota
limit for their namespace

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.090
and just kind of ring fence
them in terms of usage.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:53.840
So how would you overcommit
resources on the cluster?

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:57.330
So one way to do so,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.430
is on your container.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:04.270
For your container, set your
limit higher than your request.

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:07.770
And once you do that,
you will actually now

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.380
have the ability to
promise more resources

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.030
than what's available.

00:20:12.030 --> 00:20:15.300
This is good for things like
burstable type workloads.

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:19.580
Perhaps you want to take
advantage of as much efficiency

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:21.660
as possible.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:25.380
For pods that you think
always need to run,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:26.760
always have to be
up and running,

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.260
then you basically
set your request

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.570
equal to your limit
for those containers,

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:32.550
and those will get guaranteed.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.580
Best practice is--
oftentimes most of us

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.200
don't know what the
limits and requests are.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:39.870
We don't know what
our applications

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:40.980
are going to consume.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:48.030
So start with best
effort or even burstable,

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.600
and then see how
things come along,

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:52.440
and then keep track
of pod evictions.

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.920
And when you see a particular
pod getting evicted more

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:58.860
than others, then you know
there's something wrong,

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:02.670
and perhaps take that pod
and set it as guaranteed.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:05.700
And then there could be other
pods that literally are--

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:06.930
they can handle failure.

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:09.210
They don't really need to
be running all the time.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:13.980
Set those as best effort,
so more like the batch type

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.020
workloads.

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.700
So let's move to
the demo, and I'm

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.070
going to invite David on
stage to walk through it.

00:21:25.114 --> 00:21:26.030
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: OK.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:27.320
Thanks, Alan.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:36.580
Can we switch to the
input from the PC?

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:37.210
Perfect.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:37.709
OK.

00:21:37.709 --> 00:21:40.070
I'm going to try to make this--

00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:41.200
can people see?

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:42.790
Well, it's right at the top.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:43.930
Is that large enough?

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.000
I can make it a
little bit larger.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.050
Let's start and
see how this goes.

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:53.140
So I'm going to show two demos--
one of the quota functionality

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:55.630
that Alan talked about,
and then I'll show

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:58.540
you one of resource isolation.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:03.310
So the quota demo, it's
pretty straightforward.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:04.450
Can people see this?

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:07.010
I can make it one
more level bigger if--

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:08.170
yes, no?

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:08.970
It's good enough.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:09.050
OK.

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:10.508
People are saying
it's good enough.

00:22:10.508 --> 00:22:13.000
So we're going to
create a namespace just

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.310
to run this demo in.

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:18.040
And to start with,
you'll see that there's

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:20.740
no quota associated
with the namespace.

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:22.450
By the way, this
command kubectl,

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:24.310
is the command line
client that you

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:28.340
use to interact with
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.300
And so we're saying
here, tell me

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.460
what quotas are associated
with this namespace

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:35.410
called demos that
we just created,

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.360
and it's saying there's no
quota associated with it.

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:40.030
And so let's set up a quota.

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:43.530
And the way you set up
a quota in Kubernetes,

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:46.840
in Container Engine,
is that you create

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:50.110
a yaml file that defines
a resource quota object

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:51.760
and specifies the quota.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.290
Like Alan said
before, you can set

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:57.970
a quota for the total
amount of requests,

00:22:57.970 --> 00:23:00.910
or total amount of limit,
or total number of pods

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:03.160
that are allowed in a namespace.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:05.710
So this quota is fairly simple.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:09.580
It's saying that we're going
to set a quota of 2 CPU

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:13.360
and 2 gigs of memory, maximum,
across all of the pods that are

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:16.732
going to run in this namespace.

00:23:16.732 --> 00:23:18.190
So that's what the
file looks like,

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:22.000
and then we run cube control to
push that file into the server,

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:24.940
which will create the quota.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:27.700
So now we've created the quota,
and let's see how it works.

00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:33.730
Before we do that, we'll run
this kubectl describe, which

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.850
will show us what
quota we've installed.

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:41.230
And you can see it says
that there is a quota called

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:43.810
demo quota that's associated
with the demo's namespace,

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:47.440
and there are zero resources,
zero CPU and memory

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.690
resources in use right
now, and the quota

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:53.450
is 2 CPU and 2 gigs of memory.

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.070
So first we're going to create a
pod that uses 60% of the quota,

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.710
and that should succeed
because you're allowed

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:02.540
to use up to 100% of a quota.

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:06.730
This is the yaml
definition of a pod.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:08.470
It's very simple.

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:13.570
It's saying that the pod should
run in the demo namespace,

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:15.174
or the demo's namespace.

00:24:15.174 --> 00:24:17.340
I don't know if I highlight
stuff if you can see it.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:18.790
Yeah, I guess you probably can.

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:20.740
That's what this line is doing.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:22.450
The pod has a name,
and it's saying

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:27.130
that we're going to run this
very simple test pod called

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:29.920
hostname that just is a server
that serves the hostname if you

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:32.230
connect to it in this pod.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:35.320
And then we're setting
these resource requirements

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:36.850
on the pod.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:41.200
Like Alan said before, you can
associate limits and requests

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:42.670
with your containers.

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:44.710
Here we're just going to
associate a limit with

00:24:44.710 --> 00:24:47.620
the container because Kubernetes
defaults the request to be

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:49.450
equal to the limit if
you don't specify it,

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:51.736
and it fits better on
the screen if we don't.

00:24:51.736 --> 00:24:53.110
But what will
happen is that when

00:24:53.110 --> 00:24:55.480
we create this, both the
limits and the request

00:24:55.480 --> 00:25:01.570
will be set to 1.2 CPU and 1.2
gigs of memory, which again,

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.690
is 60% of the quota.

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:08.500
So now that was just showing you
what the yaml file looks like.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:14.860
Now we actually ask
kubectl to create that pod,

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:17.140
and it says the pod was created.

00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:19.520
So it was created successfully.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:21.990
We can run kubectl describe.

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:23.930
And there is tons
of output here,

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:29.470
but you can see that it says
that the pod is running.

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:33.610
And you can see that
the limits are 1.2.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:36.960
By the way, these units,
the M is Milli CPU,

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:41.140
and the M for the memory
is also like millibytes.

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:47.050
I'm not sure that that's
as useful as Milli CPUs.

00:25:47.050 --> 00:25:49.750
But anyway, it's showing
that the limits are

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:53.260
1.2 CPU and 1.2 gigs of
memory, and the request

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:59.260
is 1.2 CPU and 1.2 gigs of
memory, and the pod is running.

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.510
By the way, all of the
demos that I'm showing

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:03.700
are running against
a live cluster.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:08.380
Obviously the typing part
has been pre-scripted,

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:09.940
but everything that
we're doing here

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:14.500
is actually running
against a live cluster.

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:17.912
So that was just to show
you the pod is running.

00:26:17.912 --> 00:26:19.870
And now we can try to
create another pod that's

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:21.820
also going to use
60% of the quota,

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:24.944
and this should fail because
then 120% of the quota

00:26:24.944 --> 00:26:25.610
would be in use.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:27.820
So this is another pod.

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:31.170
It has a different name, but
it's otherwise identical.

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:35.540
Has same resource limits and
same resource requirements.

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:40.400
So we ask Kubernetes
to create this pod,

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:41.570
and then it gives an error.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:45.290
It says, you tried to create
this pod but it was forbidden

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:48.300
because it exceeded the quota.

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:53.060
You requested such and such
amount of CPU and memory,

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:55.460
and then it tells you how
much was already in use

00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:58.400
and what the quota limit was.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:02.090
And so then you
can see why it was

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:04.670
rejected for using more than--

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:06.440
it would have used
more than the limit

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.510
if the system had
allowed you to create it.

00:27:13.165 --> 00:27:15.640
Oh, and this is just to show
that the pod was actually not

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:16.139
created.

00:27:16.139 --> 00:27:18.400
We do kubectl describe
to show us the pod,

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:20.860
and the pod is not
found because it

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:23.020
was rejected at
creation time due to it

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:25.330
would have exceeded the quota.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.550
So then the last piece
is just to show you

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:31.900
that we can create a pod
that uses 30% of the quota

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:35.230
because then a total
of 90% will be in use.

00:27:35.230 --> 00:27:38.530
So this is, again, a third pod
identical to the first two,

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:39.760
except the limits--

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:45.280
you can see down at the bottom,
0.6 CPU, 0.6 gigs of memory.

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:46.090
We create that.

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:48.640
It says it was
successfully created.

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:50.590
And then when we run
kubectl describe,

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:56.320
you can see that it's running
and has the requests and limits

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:00.400
that we specified.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:05.230
And lastly, we can ask
Kubernetes to tell us

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:10.870
how much quota is in use by
using this kubectl describe

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:14.320
on the quota object,
and that tells us

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:16.394
both how much is in
use and how much is

00:28:16.394 --> 00:28:17.560
the maximum amount of quota.

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:20.590
So since we asked
for 60% and then 30%,

00:28:20.590 --> 00:28:25.780
you can see 90% of the CPU quota
and 90% of the memory quota

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:27.250
are in use.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:30.430
So that's a pretty
simple demo just

00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:35.240
to show you how you can set
resource quotas on namespaces,

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:39.550
the namespace granularity
for limit, for request.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:42.730
I didn't show number of pods,
but you can also set quotas

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:48.490
on the number of pods that
can be created in a namespace

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.560
to limit that resource as well.

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:55.066
So I'm going to shift to
a second resource demo.

00:28:55.066 --> 00:28:56.440
And then after
that, Alan's going

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:59.620
to continue with the
next part of the talk.

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:04.200
The second resource related
demo that I'm going to show you

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:06.520
will be about
resource isolation,

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:12.160
which is one of the things
that Alan talked about.

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:17.950
The way that Kubernetes is able
to guarantee that containers

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:20.530
that have that guaranteed
quality of service

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:22.450
are able to run
and not be evicted

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.580
is that they constrain
the amount of resources

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.190
that other containers
can use, and that

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:28.590
is done based on the limit.

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:31.690
So as Alan mentioned,
the request

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:35.172
is the amount of resources
that you tell the scheduler

00:29:35.172 --> 00:29:37.630
that you need, and that amount
is always guaranteed to you.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:40.060
It always puts the
pod on a machine

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:43.030
that has enough
resources to guarantee

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:45.160
that that request is satisfied.

00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:47.710
And then the limit can be
higher than the request

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:50.350
if you want to do
overcommitment.

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:53.350
But the limit is where
the system essentially

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:53.950
cuts you off.

00:29:53.950 --> 00:29:56.830
Like if you exceed
your memory limit,

00:29:56.830 --> 00:29:59.680
then the system will
kill your container.

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:03.850
If you exceed the CPU limit,
it will throttle the container.

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:06.370
And these enforcement
mechanisms are the way

00:30:06.370 --> 00:30:08.860
that the system can then
provide the guarantees

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:11.320
to the guaranteed
quality of service pods.

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:16.580
So I'm going to do a quick
demo to show you that.

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:21.970
We're going to, again, create
a namespace for this demo,

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:28.150
and we're going to run a
container that just turns up

00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:30.490
a bunch of memory and CPU.

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:34.600
So what we're doing here, I'll
explain what this is doing.

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:41.200
It's going to set the limits to
0.5 CPU and 200 megs of memory,

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:44.590
and then the amount of resources
that it's actually going to use

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:48.830
is going to be 150 megs
of memory and 2 CPUs.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:50.660
So for this first
part of the demo,

00:30:50.660 --> 00:30:56.110
it'll be a container
that is using

00:30:56.110 --> 00:31:04.180
less memory than the limit
but more CPU than its limit.

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:06.650
And you'll see what happens.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.700
So first, let's do
kubectl describe

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:12.010
to verify that it's running.

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:13.450
And we can see
that it's running,

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:20.050
and the limits and requests
are 500 Milli CPU--

00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.730
so 0.5 CPU.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:25.480
And also you see
the memory there.

00:31:25.480 --> 00:31:28.780
And then we can look
at the container usage.

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:33.070
So unfortunately, this takes
a minute or so to start up.

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:35.860
But in a minute, once
this has started up,

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:40.360
you'll see that the actual
usage, the CPU usage,

00:31:40.360 --> 00:31:42.550
is constrained to
be within the limit.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:46.540
So just to recap again how
this container was configured,

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:50.350
it's going to try to use 2 CPU.

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:54.175
That's what this dash
CPU's 2 is saying.

00:31:54.175 --> 00:31:56.050
It's going to try to
use 2 CPU, but the limit

00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:57.340
is set at 0.5 CPU.

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:04.750
And so the resource
isolation in the kernel level

00:32:04.750 --> 00:32:06.964
should prevent it from
using more than 0.5 CPUs.

00:32:06.964 --> 00:32:08.880
So hopefully this is
working now, which it is.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:09.550
[COUGHS]

00:32:09.550 --> 00:32:11.680
Pardon, me.

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:14.665
And you can see that
it's using 500 Milli

00:32:14.665 --> 00:32:17.251
CPU and the full
amount of memory

00:32:17.251 --> 00:32:19.250
it requested, because
that was within its limit.

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:23.420
But the CPU is being
constrained to 500 Milli CPU.

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:25.820
This command, by the way,
that I ran, this kubectl top,

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:27.620
it's a very cool command.

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:32.120
It will show you the resource
usage of your containers

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:33.450
running in the cluster.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:35.750
And so like Alan was
talking before about how

00:32:35.750 --> 00:32:37.910
if you want to provide
good resource isolation,

00:32:37.910 --> 00:32:41.510
it's useful to provide these
resource requests and limits,

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:44.150
but you may not know the first
time you run your application

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:45.020
how to set those.

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:47.570
And so this kubectl
top command is

00:32:47.570 --> 00:32:53.750
very useful for identifying how
much resources your container

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:56.332
is using, and you can
watch that over time.

00:32:56.332 --> 00:32:58.040
You can also, of
course, use a monitoring

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:00.411
tool like Stackdriver
and Container Engine,

00:33:00.411 --> 00:33:01.910
or something like
that, and then use

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:04.310
that to set the
requests and limits.

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:08.900
But anyway, that's what
this kubectl top command is.

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:16.910
So then the second
thing that I'll show--

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:22.250
that was showing how limits
can limit the CPU consumption

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:27.735
by throttling if the container
tries to exceed the CPU limit.

00:33:27.735 --> 00:33:29.360
And then the second
part is to show you

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:31.820
that if you try to
exceed your memory limit,

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:34.470
then your container will
get killed by the system.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:38.120
So here we're going to
run another container.

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:40.070
We deleted the first one.

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:47.120
And this one we're setting
the memory limit at 200 meg,

00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:50.390
and we're going to use 250 meg.

00:33:53.247 --> 00:33:54.830
In other words, the
container is going

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:58.700
to try to use more memory
than specified in the limit.

00:33:58.700 --> 00:34:02.690
And you can see here when
we do the kubectl get pods,

00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:06.980
it shows that the container
was killed due to exceeding

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:09.250
its memory limit.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:13.500
This says restarts one because
Kubernetes will automatically--

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:16.460
this container was configured
to automatically restart

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:23.360
on failure, and so it's going
to restart the container.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:26.510
Sometimes people want
containers to restart

00:34:26.510 --> 00:34:30.260
even if they use
too much memory,

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:32.420
because they know they
have a memory leak

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:35.580
and they just want the system
to periodically restart it,

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:37.880
which is one way to
deal with memory leaks.

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:42.139
And so you can also set
the container to not

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:44.179
restart in this condition.

00:34:44.179 --> 00:34:46.429
But anyway, this is just
showing that now it's

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:47.840
restarted a second time.

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:50.690
Again, it's showing you
the status was that it

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:54.350
exceeded the memory limit.

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:56.989
And we do it a third time, and
now it's in this crash loop

00:34:56.989 --> 00:35:02.090
back off state, which is to
prevent the system from just--

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:03.620
like all the resources
on the system

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.121
from being consumed, restarting
the container over and over

00:35:06.121 --> 00:35:06.620
again.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:09.290
It notices that it's killed
it a couple of times already,

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:12.440
and then it will
continue restarting it

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:16.820
but at a slower and
slower rate to prevent

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:20.360
consuming all the resources
on just doing restarts.

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:22.640
And here we caught
it on the third try.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:23.882
It's in the running state.

00:35:23.882 --> 00:35:24.590
You get the idea.

00:35:27.321 --> 00:35:29.570
This is just showing that
you can do kubectl describe.

00:35:29.570 --> 00:35:30.950
And it will show you--

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:32.900
like if you walk
up to the system

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:34.860
and you wonder why the
site's not running,

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.220
you can see that it
says that the last state

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:40.580
of this container was
a terminated state.

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:43.180
And the reason was that it
was killed due to exceeding

00:35:43.180 --> 00:35:45.800
the memory limit.

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:49.400
So that's the end of the demo.

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:53.600
That is just showing you that
we use resource isolation

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:56.000
techniques in the
kernel to limit

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:59.862
the resource consumption for
memory and CPU of containers.

00:35:59.862 --> 00:36:01.820
And the reason that's
useful is so that then we

00:36:01.820 --> 00:36:06.110
can provide the guarantees
to the other containers that

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:08.350
are requesting resources.

00:36:08.350 --> 00:36:09.090
Yeah.

00:36:09.090 --> 00:36:13.380
So I'll let Alan come
back up and continue

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:15.534
with the next part of the talk.

00:36:15.534 --> 00:36:16.700
ALAN NAIM: Thank you, David.

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:17.530
Awesome demo.

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:18.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:24.575
Let's switch back to the slides.

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:31.590
So there's also this
resource or object

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:33.930
that we have in Kubernetes
called initial resource.

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:37.920
Originally I talked
about the case

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.530
where you're
deploying a container,

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:43.860
but you actually don't know
how much to set your request

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:45.300
or how much to set your limit.

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:48.420
Well, you can take advantage
of this initial resource, which

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:53.550
actually is learning how
your container is utilizing

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:57.390
resources and will set
the appropriate request

00:36:57.390 --> 00:37:00.030
and limit for your container.

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:06.470
This is only available today
in Kubernetes open source,

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:08.507
but it will make its way into--

00:37:08.507 --> 00:37:09.840
it's currently an alpha feature.

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:14.560
It will make its way into
Container Engine shortly.

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:16.530
So the idea here
is think of a world

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:19.710
where you have
containers that you're

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:21.360
provisioning and scheduling.

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:23.880
You're taking advantage
of initial resources

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:26.640
to do some learning around
resource utilization,

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:31.620
and then you plug that all into
the horizontal pod auto scaler,

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:35.120
and then plug down into
the cluster auto scaling.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:38.670
So you have the system
now that's operating.

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:42.360
Everything is automated,
but it's running itself.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:47.190
And that's the goal in terms
of what a lot of customers

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:47.910
want to achieve.

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:49.800
And we provided these
patterns for you

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:54.480
to be able to start
taking steps towards that.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.270
So in terms of where we're
going with cluster resource

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:58.560
management--

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:01.020
as I mentioned earlier,
we're looking at adding disk

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.210
as a first class resource.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.570
There's some work that's
being done around GPU support

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:10.980
as well that will make its
way into Container Engine.

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:15.300
Our goal is to really
give you the ability

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.110
to extend these resources.

00:38:17.110 --> 00:38:19.230
So perhaps there are
certain resources

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:26.640
that you want to meter by or
do resource management by.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:32.010
So you could extend your API
and bring those resources along.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:33.870
Things like usage
base scheduling

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:35.820
is another area
we're looking at.

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:39.030
Dedicated nodes, so
environments where, perhaps, you

00:38:39.030 --> 00:38:42.810
have different organizations
that are doing things

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:46.770
very differently, but you'd like
to allocate n number of nodes

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:48.570
for each one of
these organizations

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:52.950
and ensure that everything
that they schedule actually

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:54.210
goes on those nodes.

00:38:54.210 --> 00:39:00.000
So you're actually getting
intra-cluster node isolation.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.380
So it's a pretty powerful
thing that we're investing in.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:09.480
Priority preemption,
that's another area,

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:14.550
and then improving the quality
of service enforcement.

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:18.410
Today, it's really
around the containers,

00:39:18.410 --> 00:39:22.110
but bring some of these
things up to the pod level.

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:25.500
And then Linux disk code is
for tracking in isolation

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:27.560
and then node
allocatable enforcements.

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:29.490
Those are areas
we're going into.

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:31.740
And if you're interested in
learning more about these,

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:35.860
please reach out to myself
and David after this talk.

00:39:35.860 --> 00:39:39.550
So let's switch gears into
identity and access management.

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:47.830
So today, Kubernetes
is 1.5, and we're

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:52.180
going to be releasing
1.6 very shortly.

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:56.800
So today, most of the identity
and access management roles

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.560
that are defined at
the project level

00:39:59.560 --> 00:40:03.880
really apply to the
Kubernetes cluster itself.

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:09.970
If you want finer grain control
around things like namespaces,

00:40:09.970 --> 00:40:14.540
that was not available in
Kubernetes 1.5 or earlier.

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:19.660
So the idea with project level
identity access management,

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:23.480
you have these roles that you
define within your project,

00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:28.170
and now you can associate
them with your clusters.

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:31.330
So for example, how we typically
see people doing things

00:40:31.330 --> 00:40:35.530
is taking these roles that
are predefined, like container

00:40:35.530 --> 00:40:39.370
developer, container admin,
container viewer, and defining

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:42.340
groups, and these groups
that map to specific teams

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:43.960
within your organization.

00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:46.990
And then specifying the
right level of permission

00:40:46.990 --> 00:40:51.340
for a Container Engine Cluster
associated with these groups

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:52.280
that you're defining.

00:40:52.280 --> 00:40:55.150
So in this example, you
have a developer group

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:59.740
that maps to container developer
IM role that maps to a dev

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:02.140
project, and you set
the specific permissions

00:41:02.140 --> 00:41:03.100
around that.

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:06.450
So that was available today.

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:10.510
In Kubernetes 1.6,
we're introducing

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:14.140
this concept of role
based access control down

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:15.880
to the namespace level.

00:41:15.880 --> 00:41:19.630
So David will come up on stage
in a minute or so to walk you

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:20.800
through a demo here.

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:24.130
But what he'll be
showing you is basically

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:28.030
the ability to have an
admin come along, reach out

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:32.650
to John who is on the blue team,
and then basically provide John

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.500
with access to the
blue team namespace.

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:39.380
Come along and Lisa, who
is on the green team,

00:41:39.380 --> 00:41:43.150
ensure that Lisa only
has read/write access

00:41:43.150 --> 00:41:44.980
to the green team namespace.

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:46.750
And you can even take
it one step further

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:51.400
and say, well, I want Lisa
to have actually view access

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:53.230
into the blue team namespace.

00:41:53.230 --> 00:41:55.390
So as you can see, that
level of granularity

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:57.130
is actually improved
in terms of what

00:41:57.130 --> 00:42:02.920
you can do in really connecting
service accounts, and users,

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:08.210
and roles to the underlying
resources within a cluster.

00:42:08.210 --> 00:42:11.200
And eventually we want to
take that even further.

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:14.800
So without further ado, I
will hand it back to David

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:17.740
for a namespace
access control demo.

00:42:26.784 --> 00:42:27.700
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: OK.

00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:28.440
Great.

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:33.697
So in this demo, we're
going to use three windows.

00:42:33.697 --> 00:42:35.280
Because as Alan
mentioned, we're going

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:38.820
to show how you can have
an administrator, and then

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:42.000
this blue user, and
this green user,

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:44.850
and they all have different
access permissions.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.280
So hopefully it
will be clear enough

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:50.910
when I'm jumping around
between the windows.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:53.670
And also, one thing
I wanted to mention

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:56.790
is that the different
users are going

00:42:56.790 --> 00:42:59.460
to be based on having
different service accounts.

00:42:59.460 --> 00:43:02.760
It's a little tricky
on a single machine

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:05.020
to be logged in as multiple
users at the same time.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:08.340
So what I'll show you is
based on these users having

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:10.410
different service accounts
associated with them,

00:43:10.410 --> 00:43:13.580
but in the real world
you can also just

00:43:13.580 --> 00:43:15.330
have them be based on
users without having

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:17.760
to create service accounts.

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:22.320
So the first window here
is going to be the admin.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:30.420
And they're going to run
this Gcloud command to ask

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:33.480
the Google Cloud Platform IAM
service to create a service

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:36.960
account for the blue dev.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:38.950
You don't have to worry
about the details here.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:41.190
It's just saying create
a service account

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:45.090
and then fetch the
key into a local file.

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:49.770
We're going to do the same
thing for the green team dev,

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:55.860
create a service account called
green team dev at such and such

00:43:55.860 --> 00:44:01.680
and such, and then fetch
the key into a local file.

00:44:01.680 --> 00:44:08.850
Then we're going to go over
here into the blue team window--

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:10.720
sorry, the windows
aren't color coded,

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:13.650
but I'll tell you what
I'm switching to--

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:18.180
and fetch configure kubectl
to use the credentials

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:20.920
that we just set up for
the blue service account.

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:23.310
This is all just
setup stuff so far.

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:24.300
Last bit of setup--

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:27.780
we're going to go into the
third window for the green user

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:35.230
and fetch the data into
the kubectl config file

00:44:35.230 --> 00:44:38.610
so that now the green
user has the credentials

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:41.040
for the green service account.

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:43.720
So now we can actually start
doing interesting things.

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:46.170
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:50.580
is to create a namespace
that's called blue.

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:56.250
And what you'll see is that
the blue team dev shouldn't

00:44:56.250 --> 00:45:00.660
have any access
to that namespace

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:03.460
yet because we haven't
explicitly granted permissions.

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:06.390
So we're in the second
window now, the blue window,

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:09.570
and we do kubectl get
pods, namespace blue,

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:13.290
and you can see that the
server gives an error saying

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:15.750
that the blue user
is not allowed

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:19.890
to do this operation
on the blue namespace.

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:23.550
But we can now go back to
the admins window-- oh sorry,

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.055
I didn't make these larger.

00:45:26.055 --> 00:45:28.470
I probably should have
asked about that earlier.

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:30.910
Luckily you didn't
miss too much.

00:45:30.910 --> 00:45:33.270
That might be too
big-- but anyway.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:35.760
So we go back to
the admin window,

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:43.500
and now we can do the fun part
and give the blue user access

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:47.760
to the blue namespace and using
the role-based access control

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:48.300
mechanism.

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:50.505
So the role-based
access control mechanism

00:45:50.505 --> 00:45:52.590
is based on the concept
of cluster roles,

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:54.690
and we have a number
of predefined cluster

00:45:54.690 --> 00:46:00.730
roles in Container Engine, like
admin, edit, view, and so on.

00:46:00.730 --> 00:46:02.670
This is just
showing you the ones

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:05.160
that the system comes with.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:07.380
And associated with each
of these cluster roles

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:12.250
is a set of permissions for each
type of object in the system.

00:46:12.250 --> 00:46:14.670
So this is just like a
dozen lines from the file.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:17.980
The file's much longer, but
it doesn't fit on the screen.

00:46:17.980 --> 00:46:21.960
So I'm just showing you
part of the definition

00:46:21.960 --> 00:46:25.120
of what the admin cluster
role has access to.

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:28.200
So you can see here, it
has a list of resources

00:46:28.200 --> 00:46:30.030
and sub-resources
associated with pods,

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:32.820
and a set of verbs
that are allowed

00:46:32.820 --> 00:46:36.440
for someone with
the admin access

00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:40.650
to do on those resources, like
create, delete, do a watch,

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:41.220
and so on.

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:49.050
So that was a cluster role, in
particular, the admin cluster

00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:49.590
role.

00:46:49.590 --> 00:46:50.580
And now what we're
going to do is

00:46:50.580 --> 00:46:52.640
we're going to associate
that admin cluster

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:54.820
role with the blue user.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:58.920
And the way that's done is by
creating a role binding object.

00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:01.500
This looks a little complicated,
but it's not too bad.

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:05.400
What it's saying here is that
the user called the blue team

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.460
dev is going to get
the admin cluster

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:13.490
role in the blue namespace.

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:14.870
So that's what this is doing.

00:47:14.870 --> 00:47:17.664
It's binding the blue user,
in particular their service

00:47:17.664 --> 00:47:19.580
account, because we're
using service accounts.

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:24.860
But conceptually, it's the
blue user to the admin cluster

00:47:24.860 --> 00:47:26.960
role in the blue namespace.

00:47:26.960 --> 00:47:31.830
So now we ask the server
to create that binding.

00:47:31.830 --> 00:47:34.670
And now we can go back to the
window with the blue user,

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:37.220
where previously, we saw
that they didn't have access

00:47:37.220 --> 00:47:38.580
to the blue namespace.

00:47:38.580 --> 00:47:40.850
And now when they
do kubectl get pods,

00:47:40.850 --> 00:47:42.680
they get no resources found.

00:47:42.680 --> 00:47:43.790
That's what we'd expect.

00:47:43.790 --> 00:47:46.130
We haven't created
any pods yet, but you

00:47:46.130 --> 00:47:48.410
see they're not getting the
permission denied error.

00:47:48.410 --> 00:47:52.700
They're just being told that
the resources are not available

00:47:52.700 --> 00:47:53.960
yet.

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:56.690
And they can do other things.

00:47:56.690 --> 00:47:57.680
They can get services.

00:47:57.680 --> 00:47:59.180
We haven't created
any services yet.

00:47:59.180 --> 00:48:00.320
That's not too exciting.

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:02.600
They can run kubectl
run which creates

00:48:02.600 --> 00:48:04.670
a deployment that
starts up a Nginx

00:48:04.670 --> 00:48:08.690
server in the blue namespace.

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:12.050
So now, like I said, you can
see that they have permissions

00:48:12.050 --> 00:48:14.510
in the blue namespace.

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:17.990
So now let's go back
to the Admin user,

00:48:17.990 --> 00:48:21.890
and we're going to set up the
permissions for a green user.

00:48:21.890 --> 00:48:26.600
So first, we create
a green user--

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:34.340
sorry, the green namespace,
and then another role binding.

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:39.500
This time the user is
the green team dev,

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:41.150
but it's the same permissions.

00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:46.010
It's the admin cluster role.

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:47.930
It's being bound to
the green namespace.

00:48:47.930 --> 00:48:50.180
So this is identical to the
last role binding we saw,

00:48:50.180 --> 00:48:52.330
but instead of the blue
user, it's the green user,

00:48:52.330 --> 00:48:53.930
and instead of the
blue namespace,

00:48:53.930 --> 00:48:55.580
it's the green namespace.

00:48:55.580 --> 00:48:58.180
And so let's create that.

00:48:58.180 --> 00:49:00.890
And after we create that,
before we go and look

00:49:00.890 --> 00:49:04.280
at what the green user can do,
let's go back to the blue user

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:06.590
and just verify that
they can't do anything

00:49:06.590 --> 00:49:08.420
in this green namespace.

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:14.120
So we do kubectl get pods
in the green namespace.

00:49:14.120 --> 00:49:15.770
You can see that
that's forbidden.

00:49:15.770 --> 00:49:18.020
It gets services.

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:20.030
They can't see that
and they can't request

00:49:20.030 --> 00:49:22.070
to see all namespaces either.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.160
So the blue user,
as we intended,

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.350
doesn't have access in
the green namespace.

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:31.760
But we can go over to
the third window, which

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:35.000
is the one where we have
the green user set up,

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:39.710
and you can see that
they have permission

00:49:39.710 --> 00:49:40.830
in the green namespace.

00:49:40.830 --> 00:49:42.590
It's get pods, namespace green.

00:49:42.590 --> 00:49:43.820
It says no resources found.

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:45.194
We haven't created
any resources,

00:49:45.194 --> 00:49:47.360
but this shows they
have permission

00:49:47.360 --> 00:49:49.640
to look at the resources.

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:52.580
They can't look in
the blue namespace,

00:49:52.580 --> 00:49:54.680
because we didn't
give them permission

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:56.420
to look in the blue
namespace, but they

00:49:56.420 --> 00:50:01.190
do have permission in
the green namespace.

00:50:01.190 --> 00:50:06.100
They can run Nginx or
do whatever they want

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:09.380
inside the green namespace.

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:13.340
So now we have the blue
user with full permission

00:50:13.340 --> 00:50:16.110
in the blue namespace, the
green user with full permission

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:17.900
in the green namespace.

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:21.450
And I'll show you
one last piece,

00:50:21.450 --> 00:50:25.610
which is that view thing that
Alan mentioned a minute ago.

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:28.550
Let's say hypothetically that
the green user, in addition

00:50:28.550 --> 00:50:31.490
to having permissions
to do whatever they want

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:33.110
in their green
namespace, also is

00:50:33.110 --> 00:50:34.700
supposed to have
view permissions

00:50:34.700 --> 00:50:35.630
in the blue namespace.

00:50:35.630 --> 00:50:37.850
Maybe they're allowed to
monitor the objects that

00:50:37.850 --> 00:50:40.640
are created by that blue user.

00:50:40.640 --> 00:50:42.980
And so what we can do is that--

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:45.350
oh sorry, well, first
we'll show that the admin

00:50:45.350 --> 00:50:49.220
user can see all the resources
in all of the namespaces.

00:50:49.220 --> 00:50:52.070
So some of these are system
resources at the bottom,

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:53.600
but you can see
the top two lines

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:57.980
are the Nginx deployment
that we set up

00:50:57.980 --> 00:51:01.260
that the blue user started and
that the green user started.

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:03.260
So now onto what I was
just saying a second ago.

00:51:03.260 --> 00:51:05.100
We're going to create
a third role binding.

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:06.850
This is going to give
the view permission.

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:09.550
You can see down here,
cluster roll view.

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:13.870
It's going to give the view
permission to the green team

00:51:13.870 --> 00:51:18.530
user, give them view permission
on the blue namespace.

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:22.910
So we create this role binding.

00:51:22.910 --> 00:51:30.890
We can go back to the
window for the green user.

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:32.930
Do get deployments,
namespace blue,

00:51:32.930 --> 00:51:38.870
and you can see that this
green user can view objects,

00:51:38.870 --> 00:51:42.500
in particular, the deployment
objects in the blue namespace,

00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:44.150
but they can't do
other mutations.

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:48.086
For example, this green user
can't do this kubectl Delete

00:51:48.086 --> 00:51:49.460
and try to delete
the deployment,

00:51:49.460 --> 00:51:54.030
because they only have
view access to it.

00:51:54.030 --> 00:51:58.160
And so that's pretty
much the end of the demo.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:01.070
Just showing again that
you can give fairly fine

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:06.320
grained access control to users
on a namespace granularity.

00:52:06.320 --> 00:52:08.780
Giving them
permissions to create,

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:11.870
delete, view
objects, and a bunch

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:14.390
of different other
operations based

00:52:14.390 --> 00:52:19.400
on what permissions
you're trying to set up.

00:52:19.400 --> 00:52:20.900
So I'll hand it
back to you, Alan.

00:52:24.240 --> 00:52:25.560
ALAN NAIM: Thank you.

00:52:25.560 --> 00:52:29.070
So putting it all together
to summarize everything we've

00:52:29.070 --> 00:52:30.390
talked about today so far.

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:36.430
Recommendation is break out
your operating environment,

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:37.850
in particular, production.

00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:39.880
It's a separate project.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:44.320
And then put your dev test
staging in another project.

00:52:44.320 --> 00:52:47.620
And depending on the
requirements for your teams,

00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:51.495
you could put them same cluster
or separate dev test staging

00:52:51.495 --> 00:52:52.495
into different clusters.

00:52:55.250 --> 00:53:01.895
Namespaces are a great boundary,
especially in Kubernetes 1.6

00:53:01.895 --> 00:53:05.130
for having user level control.

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:08.000
So for cases where
you're running a cluster

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:09.860
with multiple
applications, you'll

00:53:09.860 --> 00:53:14.570
be able to associate roles
with these underlying

00:53:14.570 --> 00:53:16.910
namespace objects.

00:53:16.910 --> 00:53:21.590
Leverage groups to encapsulate
the actual identity access

00:53:21.590 --> 00:53:23.890
management roles.

00:53:23.890 --> 00:53:26.630
You can create these groups
that actually map back

00:53:26.630 --> 00:53:30.250
to your processes, and then
that's really the benefit

00:53:30.250 --> 00:53:33.140
that they provide you.

00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:38.870
Leverage Kubernetes namespaces
and assign resource quotas

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:42.860
for cases where you have
different departments,

00:53:42.860 --> 00:53:45.560
different requirements
that are sharing resources,

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:48.650
and potentially, you
could have abuse.

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:53.480
So leverage these quotas to
actually avoid these situations

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:54.800
from happening.

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:58.760
And in cases where
you are running

00:53:58.760 --> 00:54:01.880
heterogeneous applications
within a cluster, where you're

00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:04.460
sharing cluster
resources, really start

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:07.760
thinking about using
requests and limits.

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:11.820
A scenario that not too many
people really understand,

00:54:11.820 --> 00:54:13.580
but I think this is
something definitely

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:17.900
worth looking at and
using in the future,

00:54:17.900 --> 00:54:20.570
actually, to provide the
best quality of service

00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:23.660
for your applications as they
run within the environments.

00:54:23.660 --> 00:54:27.500
And then take advantage
of some of these objects,

00:54:27.500 --> 00:54:30.920
like initial resources, to
help you better understand

00:54:30.920 --> 00:54:34.770
usage and utilization
for your containers.

00:54:34.770 --> 00:54:38.310
That being said, here's
some links and resources.

00:54:38.310 --> 00:54:44.070
There's much more we can
provide you, but the idea is--

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:45.910
for those of you that
are new to Kubernetes,

00:54:45.910 --> 00:54:48.630
there's some phenomenal
training courses

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:50.670
that are available online.

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:54.920
And our documentation goes into
pretty much a lot of detail

00:54:54.920 --> 00:54:57.840
as far as request
limits and using

00:54:57.840 --> 00:55:00.900
those types of
quality of service

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:03.840
controls for your cluster.

00:55:03.840 --> 00:55:07.410
That being said, I
will open it up to Q&amp;A.

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:10.150
And perhaps, David, you
want to come back up,

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:12.127
and we'll take your questions.

00:55:16.679 --> 00:55:17.220
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:19.950
So I had a quick question about
the role-based access control

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:22.610
that you had shown in
the previous slide.

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:24.501
I saw that you use
service accounts.

00:55:24.501 --> 00:55:26.750
Does it also work with user
accounts and Google Groups

00:55:26.750 --> 00:55:27.840
if you use that?

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:28.130
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Yeah.

00:55:28.130 --> 00:55:29.297
It works it works with user.

00:55:29.297 --> 00:55:30.921
It definitely works
with user accounts.

00:55:30.921 --> 00:55:33.020
Like I said, the only
reason we were using service

00:55:33.020 --> 00:55:35.145
accounts there was because
it's hard to kind of log

00:55:35.145 --> 00:55:37.270
in from multiple users
at the same time.

00:55:37.270 --> 00:55:38.870
AUDIENCE: And this
is in 1.6, right?

00:55:38.870 --> 00:55:41.324
And you were using it
against the cluster,

00:55:41.324 --> 00:55:42.740
so the cluster is
upgraded to 1.6?

00:55:42.740 --> 00:55:43.656
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: No.

00:55:43.656 --> 00:55:46.580
To be clear-- that's
a great question.

00:55:46.580 --> 00:55:48.800
I was running against
a test cluster,

00:55:48.800 --> 00:55:52.730
like an internal test
cluster that we have up.

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:54.750
So it's not available yet.

00:55:54.750 --> 00:56:00.409
1.6 will be rolled out sometime
in the next month or so.

00:56:00.409 --> 00:56:00.950
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:56:00.950 --> 00:56:03.440
Thank you.

00:56:03.440 --> 00:56:07.330
AUDIENCE: For the instances
that are running on best effort

00:56:07.330 --> 00:56:09.350
with overcommitment
of resources,

00:56:09.350 --> 00:56:10.940
before something
would be evicted,

00:56:10.940 --> 00:56:13.520
is there any type
of notification that

00:56:13.520 --> 00:56:16.190
would happen for the workload
to say, hey, prepare myself

00:56:16.190 --> 00:56:17.180
to go away?

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:19.884
Any type of warning, or would
it be an immediate eviction?

00:56:19.884 --> 00:56:20.800
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: No.

00:56:20.800 --> 00:56:27.320
For the memory eviction, right
now there's no notification.

00:56:27.320 --> 00:56:30.860
It's the system immediately
needs to reclaim the memory,

00:56:30.860 --> 00:56:33.110
and so you get
evicted right away.

00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:37.220
There's other kinds of
preemption type operations

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:40.430
in Kubernetes, like when
you're doing a rolling update

00:56:40.430 --> 00:56:44.600
and you're restarting your
pod into a new container image

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:47.960
version and things like that,
where we do provide notice.

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:50.236
But this is kind of
the one situation

00:56:50.236 --> 00:56:51.860
where there really
isn't an opportunity

00:56:51.860 --> 00:56:56.282
to run some other code to
notify your application,

00:56:56.282 --> 00:56:58.490
because there just isn't
any memory left on the node,

00:56:58.490 --> 00:57:03.027
and so we kill it
without notification.

00:57:03.027 --> 00:57:03.610
AUDIENCE: Hey.

00:57:03.610 --> 00:57:06.280
So you guys didn't
set any limits

00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:09.110
on the amount of questions
that can be asked, right?

00:57:09.110 --> 00:57:09.610
OK.

00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:12.249
DAVID OPPENHEIMER:
Very good pun, yes.

00:57:12.249 --> 00:57:12.790
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:57:12.790 --> 00:57:15.250
So I just have a few.

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:18.010
So the IAM stuff, it
only works in GCP, right?

00:57:20.199 --> 00:57:21.490
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Not exactly.

00:57:21.490 --> 00:57:25.300
So the fundamental role-based
access control mechanism

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:27.130
is part of open
source Kubernetes.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:30.160
The tie-in into the
Google Cloud Platform

00:57:30.160 --> 00:57:34.922
IAM, that's how we were
using the GCP service

00:57:34.922 --> 00:57:36.130
accounts and stuff like that.

00:57:36.130 --> 00:57:39.330
That part is only on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:57:39.330 --> 00:57:42.160
but all of the
role-based access control

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:44.170
mechanisms, the
underlying stuff,

00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:46.019
is all in the open source.

00:57:46.019 --> 00:57:46.560
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:57:46.560 --> 00:57:50.150
So it already works
in the AWS today?

00:57:50.150 --> 00:57:53.560
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: I
don't know how connected--

00:57:53.560 --> 00:57:55.750
if people have done that
last mile of plumbing

00:57:55.750 --> 00:57:59.980
to connect it to the
AWS IAM mechanism.

00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:02.710
We have a special
interest group, Kubernetes

00:58:02.710 --> 00:58:06.580
special interest
group for AWS, and you

00:58:06.580 --> 00:58:08.964
can ask a question there.

00:58:08.964 --> 00:58:10.630
I don't know the
answer to the question,

00:58:10.630 --> 00:58:13.540
but in principle,
it's possible, yeah.

00:58:13.540 --> 00:58:14.980
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:58:14.980 --> 00:58:19.570
Also, are there any plans on
more granular authorization?

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:22.060
So I know you guys do the
namespace authorization.

00:58:22.060 --> 00:58:23.710
It seems like that's
the lowest you go.

00:58:23.710 --> 00:58:27.520
Are there any plans on not
allowing certain aspects

00:58:27.520 --> 00:58:28.900
of the API to be used?

00:58:28.900 --> 00:58:30.730
So say I don't want
someone to create

00:58:30.730 --> 00:58:33.700
a storage set in a
certain role, or I

00:58:33.700 --> 00:58:35.227
don't want them to
create something

00:58:35.227 --> 00:58:37.060
without a health check,
or I don't want them

00:58:37.060 --> 00:58:38.860
to open up a certain port--

00:58:38.860 --> 00:58:39.905
things along those lines?

00:58:39.905 --> 00:58:42.280
Or is that always going to be
above the Kubernetes level?

00:58:46.840 --> 00:58:50.290
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: I don't
have a good answer for that.

00:58:50.290 --> 00:58:52.690
Today the lowest
level of granularity

00:58:52.690 --> 00:58:57.790
is the namespace and sort of
what you can do in a namespace,

00:58:57.790 --> 00:59:05.070
but in the future, there might
be those other possibilities.

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:08.440
I'm not aware of discussions
going on yet about that,

00:59:08.440 --> 00:59:10.660
but those do seem like
reasonable use cases.

00:59:14.670 --> 00:59:17.070
AUDIENCE: For the
CPU limiting, is

00:59:17.070 --> 00:59:22.455
that done through some
combination of CPU throttling

00:59:22.455 --> 00:59:27.379
or some sort of combination of
CPU affinity and throttling?

00:59:27.379 --> 00:59:29.670
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: So the
way it works under the covers

00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:38.790
is that the CPU request maps to
CPU shares in the CFS scheduler

00:59:38.790 --> 00:59:39.930
in the Linux kernel.

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:44.910
And the CPU limit maps
to some other CPU--

00:59:44.910 --> 00:59:46.272
I don't remember the details.

00:59:46.272 --> 00:59:48.230
Something else in the
kernel that throttles it.

00:59:51.560 --> 00:59:57.540
So it's using kernel CPU
scheduling mechanisms

00:59:57.540 --> 00:59:58.859
to enforce that.

00:59:58.859 --> 00:59:59.400
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:59:59.400 --> 01:00:01.574
So you're not pinning
it to a specific CPU.

01:00:01.574 --> 01:00:02.490
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: No.

01:00:02.490 --> 01:00:07.770
We don't have support yet
for pinning containers

01:00:07.770 --> 01:00:10.470
to specific CPUs, and
that's not the mechanism

01:00:10.470 --> 01:00:11.420
that's used there.

01:00:11.420 --> 01:00:12.646
AUDIENCE: Cool, thanks.

01:00:12.646 --> 01:00:14.217
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Yeah, sure.

01:00:14.217 --> 01:00:16.300
AUDIENCE: In the beginning
you talked about people

01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:18.880
splitting things up in
terms of just like projects,

01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:21.082
or namespaces, or clusters.

01:00:21.082 --> 01:00:23.290
Some were suggesting in
production people are running

01:00:23.290 --> 01:00:25.070
services that are-- just
from my experience-- people

01:00:25.070 --> 01:00:26.986
are running services
that are kind of similar.

01:00:26.986 --> 01:00:29.186
So let's say I have a
bunch of stateful sets.

01:00:29.186 --> 01:00:31.060
I have some things that
are running the JVMs,

01:00:31.060 --> 01:00:33.904
so they need the JV memory,
or I have some Redis clusters.

01:00:33.904 --> 01:00:36.070
Are people looking at
splitting these things up just

01:00:36.070 --> 01:00:38.200
in terms of just namespaces?

01:00:38.200 --> 01:00:40.780
Or is there kind of an incentive
to split this up by cluster?

01:00:40.780 --> 01:00:42.155
Because then you
can optimize out

01:00:42.155 --> 01:00:44.800
of the parameter setting
just by cluster instead

01:00:44.800 --> 01:00:46.150
of having do it by namespace.

01:00:46.150 --> 01:00:47.937
Are there any pros
and cons with that?

01:00:47.937 --> 01:00:49.270
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: I don't know.

01:00:49.270 --> 01:00:51.170
Maybe Alan could talk.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:53.620
Do you want to take that, or
do you want me to take that?

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:54.642
ALAN NAIM: Yeah.

01:00:54.642 --> 01:00:56.620
It really depends
on your application.

01:00:56.620 --> 01:00:58.990
But what we see
out there is people

01:00:58.990 --> 01:01:02.200
are running these within
their own namespace

01:01:02.200 --> 01:01:06.610
and sharing a
particular cluster.

01:01:06.610 --> 01:01:09.880
There are cases, though,
where the applications have

01:01:09.880 --> 01:01:14.380
certain data stringent
requirements that really

01:01:14.380 --> 01:01:18.760
require true isolation from a
networking perspective as well,

01:01:18.760 --> 01:01:21.220
and those are cases where
people end up splitting them up

01:01:21.220 --> 01:01:24.080
into different clusters.

01:01:24.080 --> 01:01:25.240
You want to add to that?

01:01:25.240 --> 01:01:26.240
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Yeah.

01:01:26.240 --> 01:01:26.780
I agree.

01:01:26.780 --> 01:01:32.750
It kind of depends on what
specific kind of isolation

01:01:32.750 --> 01:01:34.460
you're trying to achieve.

01:01:34.460 --> 01:01:39.440
If you wanted to say more about
what you were trying to do,

01:01:39.440 --> 01:01:43.316
we could give you
a recommendation.

01:01:43.316 --> 01:01:45.440
You can do it now, or you
can do it after the talk.

01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:46.380
It's up to you.

01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:50.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

